Below is the complete command sequence for Jetson 6, from starting OVS and Ryu through creating seven virtual switches and connecting them with virtual Ethernet links. It reflects the setup we've already established.

Important: Your OVS daemon and Ryu controller are already running, and the seven switches and seven links have been created. The commands below are a reusable record of the process. Don't rerun the startup commands blindly on the current system; I've marked which steps are already complete.

# 1. Activate the Ryu virtual environment

Run this in Terminal 1:

Bash

```
source ~/phd/lab-env/venv/bin/activate

ryu-manager --version
```

Expected: Ryu 4.34.

# 2. Start Ryu

If Ryu is already running in Terminal 1, leave it running. Otherwise, start it with:

Bash

```
source ~/phd/lab-env/venv/bin/activate

ryu-manager ryu.app.simple_switch_13 --verbose
```

Keep this terminal open. Ryu listens on OpenFlow port `6653`.

# 3. Start or verify Open vSwitch

Open Terminal 2.

Check whether the OVS daemon is already running:

Bash

```
sudo pgrep -af ovs-vswitchd
```

If it is running, do not start another instance. In your current setup, the daemon is already running.

If it is not running, and the OVS database server is running, the previously used startup command is:

Bash

```
sudo ovs-vswitchd --pidfile --detach \
  --log-file unix:/var/run/openvswitch/db.sock
```

Check the OVS version:

Bash

```
sudo ovs-vsctl --version
```

Your installed version is OVS 2.17.12.

# 4. Create the first virtual switch

The first switch is named `br-ryu-test`, and it serves as S1.

This switch already exists in your current environment.

Bash

```
sudo ovs-vsctl --may-exist add-br br-ryu-test

sudo ovs-vsctl set bridge br-ryu-test \
  datapath_type=netdev protocols=OpenFlow13

sudo ovs-vsctl set-controller br-ryu-test \
  tcp:127.0.0.1:6653
```

# 5. Create the remaining six switches

The following commands create S2–S7 and configure each to use the userspace `netdev` datapath and OpenFlow 1.3.

These switches already exist in your current environment.

Bash

```
for br in s2 s3 s4 s5 s6 s7; do
  sudo ovs-vsctl --may-exist add-br "$br"

  sudo ovs-vsctl set bridge "$br" \
    datapath_type=netdev protocols=OpenFlow13

  sudo ovs-vsctl set-controller "$br" \
    tcp:127.0.0.1:6653
done
```

The `netdev` setting is important because your Jetson 6 kernel does not currently have the OVS kernel module available.

# 6. Verify all seven switches

Bash

```
sudo ovs-vsctl list-br
```

Expected switch names:

```
br-ryu-test
s2
s3
s4
s5
s6
s7
```

Check their datapath type, controller status and datapath IDs:

Bash

```
for br in br-ryu-test s2 s3 s4 s5 s6 s7; do
  echo "===== $br ====="

  sudo ovs-vsctl get Bridge "$br" datapath_type
  sudo ovs-vsctl get Controller "$br" is_connected
  sudo ovs-ofctl -O OpenFlow13 show "$br" | head -n 2
done
```

Expected results:

* Datapath type: `netdev`

* Controller status: `true`

* A unique DPID for every switch

We have already confirmed all three for your seven switches.

