The alternate route bypasses S3 and rejoins the main path at S4.

The following script creates seven virtual Ethernet pairs and attaches each end to the appropriate switch. You have already run this successfully, so don't rerun it unless you are rebuilding or repairing the topology.

Bash

```
sudo bash <<'EOF'
set -e

while read -r a br_a b br_b; do
    echo "Configuring $br_a <--> $br_b ($a <--> $b)"

    if ip link show "$a" >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
       ip link show "$b" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "  veth pair already exists"
    elif ! ip link show "$a" >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
         ! ip link show "$b" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        ip link add "$a" type veth peer name "$b"
    else
        echo "ERROR: only one end of the veth pair exists."
        exit 1
    fi

    ip link set "$a" up
    ip link set "$b" up

    ovs-vsctl --may-exist add-port "$br_a" "$a"
    ovs-vsctl --may-exist add-port "$br_b" "$b"
done <<'LINKS'
v12a br-ryu-test v12b s2
v23a s2 v23b s3
v34a s3 v34b s4
v47a s4 v47b s7
v25a s2 v25b s5
v56a s5 v56b s6
v64a s6 v64b s4
LINKS

echo "Virtual links configured."
EOF
```

